Let's go ahead and get started.

2
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:10,960
This time, we're
going to talk about

3
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:14,765
content distribution
networks or CDNs.

4
00:00:14,765 --> 00:00:18,705
This whole module is
about network services,

5
00:00:18,705 --> 00:00:21,885
and the first network service

6
00:00:21,885 --> 00:00:24,400
we went through is a
really important one.

7
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,100
That's load balancing.

8
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:28,320
Here is another network service

9
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:32,430
Google provides, and that's CDN.

10
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:37,400
Let's take a review of HTTP.

11
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:39,240
We showed this before when

12
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:41,260
we went through load balancing.

13
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:42,965
It's worth showing again.

14
00:00:42,965 --> 00:00:48,320
HTTP is the web's
application layer protocol,

15
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:52,640
and client and server
exchange web objects,

16
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:57,105
like HTML files,
JPEGs, and so forth.

17
00:00:57,105 --> 00:01:01,950
A PC running a browser

18
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:05,700
sends a request to the server
and it gets a response,

19
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:07,725
and that file is sent to it.

20
00:01:07,725 --> 00:01:12,050
A different device
would do request,

21
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:13,610
perhaps for the same file,

22
00:01:13,610 --> 00:01:15,450
and then get the response.

23
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:18,460
Maybe it's looking for blah.gif.

24
00:01:18,710 --> 00:01:21,840
How this looks
across the Internet?

25
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:23,490
If you recall, the Internet is

26
00:01:23,490 --> 00:01:25,500
a bunch of autonomous systems.

27
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:27,480
A client, these

28
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:29,910
are our two clients and
let's say our server,

29
00:01:29,910 --> 00:01:31,690
well, they're going to make

30
00:01:31,690 --> 00:01:34,630
their requests across
this Internet.

31
00:01:34,630 --> 00:01:37,650
It's going to traverse
many of these networks.

32
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:42,070
This leads to potentially
some poor performance,

33
00:01:42,070 --> 00:01:45,420
reliability, scalability issues.

34
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:49,370
There's a single point
of failure back here,

35
00:01:49,370 --> 00:01:53,430
easy to be overloaded,
to overload the server.

36
00:01:53,430 --> 00:01:56,630
There's a long latency
of the request.

37
00:01:56,630 --> 00:02:01,570
There's a lot of peering
traffic that's happening.

38
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:05,535
It's not a great situation.

39
00:02:05,535 --> 00:02:06,980
Now, we can alleviate
some of those,

40
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:10,830
adding extra servers
and load balancing,

41
00:02:10,830 --> 00:02:13,080
but you still don't have

42
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:15,690
all that challenge or

43
00:02:15,690 --> 00:02:18,560
those problems going
across the Internet.

44
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:22,240
What a CDN is looks like this.

45
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:29,590
It's this virtual network
where the CDN provider

46
00:02:29,590 --> 00:02:32,850
creates a network
with servers at

47
00:02:32,850 --> 00:02:35,370
the edge and a lot of

48
00:02:35,370 --> 00:02:38,930
peering agreements with
network providers.

49
00:02:38,930 --> 00:02:41,630
Then content providers,

50
00:02:41,630 --> 00:02:44,590
namely the one that's
running the server,

51
00:02:44,590 --> 00:02:47,610
signs up with the
CDN provider and

52
00:02:47,610 --> 00:02:51,150
specifies here's
my origin server.

53
00:02:51,150 --> 00:02:56,100
Client requests then
go to the CDN servers.

54
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:58,410
Completely, they don't ever

55
00:02:58,410 --> 00:03:00,835
interact directly with
the origin server.

56
00:03:00,835 --> 00:03:07,530
The client would go to its
nearest CDNs edge server.

57
00:03:07,530 --> 00:03:10,790
That edge server would
say, do I have it?

58
00:03:10,790 --> 00:03:14,055
No. It will contact
the origin server,

59
00:03:14,055 --> 00:03:17,520
and then it will
cache the content.

60
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:25,590
It may exchange it with other
servers in its network,

61
00:03:25,590 --> 00:03:27,030
but let's assume it's real

62
00:03:27,030 --> 00:03:31,170
simplistic and it
just caches it here.

63
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:33,170
Let's say this other client then

64
00:03:33,170 --> 00:03:35,550
accesses the same content.

65
00:03:35,550 --> 00:03:40,605
It will hit it directly at
this server and return.

66
00:03:40,605 --> 00:03:43,635
Because the content is cached,

67
00:03:43,635 --> 00:03:46,880
it was basically very
minimal network traffic

68
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:49,480
across this full path

69
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:52,760
and very minimal load
on the origin server.

70
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:54,705
Just enough for this guy,

71
00:03:54,705 --> 00:03:57,165
this CDN server,

72
00:03:57,165 --> 00:03:59,680
to get the content.

73
00:04:00,830 --> 00:04:06,970
Google provides
basically this service.

74
00:04:08,570 --> 00:04:10,820
Well, the three main things

75
00:04:10,820 --> 00:04:12,200
is you're going to
create the origin,

76
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:14,520
you specify what
that origin server

77
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:17,025
is or origin of the content,

78
00:04:17,025 --> 00:04:22,035
point it to some
backend service,

79
00:04:22,035 --> 00:04:28,875
and configure how it
should cache the content.

80
00:04:28,875 --> 00:04:32,070
Let's go over to our console.

81
00:04:32,070 --> 00:04:38,115
I can type in CDN and
upward Cloud CDN.

82
00:04:38,115 --> 00:04:40,160
In this, if you
wanted to find it,

83
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:45,840
it would be under network
services, those main things.

84
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:47,775
Now, the different
backends you can have,

85
00:04:47,775 --> 00:04:49,305
a backend bucket,

86
00:04:49,305 --> 00:04:51,300
so using Google storage.

87
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:54,225
We could specify
a backend service

88
00:04:54,225 --> 00:04:58,060
like that flask gap that we ran,

89
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:01,610
put some load balancers
in front of it,

90
00:05:01,610 --> 00:05:04,020
and we can say that
backend service.

91
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:06,140
I don't have that
running anymore.

92
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:09,630
Another thing you can do
is this custom origin.

93
00:05:09,630 --> 00:05:12,700
Maybe I have a server.

94
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:15,260
Let's say it's
running at my house.

95
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:17,280
I have an IP address.

96
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:21,615
I'm just going to make up
a fake one for right now,

97
00:05:21,615 --> 00:05:26,410
and it's port 8888 let's say.

98
00:05:26,750 --> 00:05:32,290
We're going to call
this Eric's origin.

99
00:05:33,530 --> 00:05:36,555
I could just point Google

100
00:05:36,555 --> 00:05:40,720
to my backend server
that I'm running.

101
00:05:41,930 --> 00:05:45,795
Then I would attach it to

102
00:05:45,795 --> 00:05:48,645
an existing load balancer

103
00:05:48,645 --> 00:05:53,100
or I can create a
new load balancer,

104
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:57,250
and they'll do it for me.

105
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:03,435
Then I can configure the cache.

106
00:06:03,435 --> 00:06:09,670
A bunch of different settings,

107
00:06:09,670 --> 00:06:14,390
and this is going to depend
on what makes sense for

108
00:06:14,390 --> 00:06:17,280
your particular web server that

109
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:20,820
you're running and all of that.

110
00:06:20,820 --> 00:06:22,990
It's pretty
straightforward, and then

